lookup cache: remove the overcommit mark before freeing the tables

A thread's lookup tables are reserved, marked as an overcommit range, and committed from the
fault handler, which looks the faulting address up in the list of marked ranges. The
destructor freed the tables first and removed the mark second. Between the two calls another
thread that is starting can be given the same address for its own tables and mark it; the
mark it adds is the one the exiting thread then removes. The new thread's first lookup faults,
the handler finds no range and passes the fault on as the program's own, and the thread never
gets out of exception handling: the program hangs with one core busy.

It takes threads starting while others exit. tests/runtime/thread-churn.c (60 rounds of 64
short threads, about a second) hung every time before this and passes with it; a benchmark
that starts 256 threads hung about once in 140 runs.

--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/LookupCache.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/LookupCache.cpp
@@ -71,8 +71,11 @@
 }
 
 LookupCache::~LookupCache() {
-  FEXCore::Allocator::VirtualFree(reinterpret_cast<void*>(PagePointer), TotalCacheSize);
+  // FineWine: unmark before freeing. The other way round, a thread that is starting can be
+  // given this address for its own tables and mark them between the two calls; the unmark then
+  // removes that thread's mark, and its first lookup faults with nothing to commit the memory.
   ctx->SyscallHandler->UnmarkOvercommitRange(PagePointer, TotalCacheSize);
+  FEXCore::Allocator::VirtualFree(reinterpret_cast<void*>(PagePointer), TotalCacheSize);
 
   // No need to free BlockLinks map.
   // These will get freed when their memory allocators are deallocated.
